    "tabs": {
      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The Arisaka Type 99 was a bolt-action service rifle adopted by the Imperial Japanese Army in 1939. Designed to replace the earlier Type 38, it was chambered for the more powerful 7.7×58mm cartridge, offering improved ballistic performance and stopping power.\n\nThe Type 99 featured a robust bolt-action system with dual locking lugs and a strong receiver, making it one of the more durable military rifles of its era. Early production models included features such as a chrome-lined bore for corrosion resistance, anti-aircraft sight wings, and a monopod for stability.\n\nAs the war progressed, manufacturing quality declined due to resource shortages and production pressures. Later rifles, often referred to as \"last ditch\" variants, were simplified significantly, sometimes lacking basic finishing and non-essential components.\n\nDespite these variations, the Type 99 remained a reliable and effective service rifle. It was widely used by Japanese forces throughout World War II in a variety of combat environments.\n\nToday, the Type 99 is valued by collectors for its historical significance and unique features, as well as its representation of wartime production changes.",
        "wikipedia": {
