    "tabs": {
      "history": {
        "title": "Firearm Origin...",
        "content": "The SKS is a semi-automatic rifle originally developed in the Soviet Union in the mid-1940s and later produced by several countries, including China. The Chinese variant, often manufactured by Norinco, became one of the most widely distributed versions of the rifle.\n\nChambered in 7.62×39mm, the SKS uses a gas-operated system with a tilting bolt. It features a fixed 10-round magazine that is typically loaded using stripper clips. The rifle was designed as a transitional infantry weapon, bridging the gap between traditional bolt-action rifles and modern assault rifles.\n\nChinese SKS rifles were produced in large numbers and exported globally, often entering civilian markets as surplus firearms. Many were modified with detachable magazines or aftermarket stocks, though original configurations remain the most desirable among collectors.\n\nThe SKS is known for its durability, simple construction, and ease of maintenance. While eventually replaced in military service by the AK-47, it remains a popular rifle for recreational shooting and collecting.\n\nToday, the SKS is appreciated for its historical significance and its role in the evolution of modern infantry firearms.",
        "wikipedia": {
          "title": "",
          "content": "",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Springfield_M1903"
